' Mockito Framework Classes
class MockitoAnnotations <<MockitoCore>> {
  +openMocks(testClass: Object): AutoCloseable
  +initMocks(testClass: Object): void
}

class Mockito <<MockitoCore>> {
  +mock(classToMock: Class<T>): T
  +spy(realObject: T): T
  +when(methodCall: T): OngoingStubbing<T>
  +verify(mock: T): T
  +verify(mock: T, mode: VerificationMode): T
  +reset(mocks: Object...): void
  +times(wantedNumberOfInvocations: int): VerificationMode
  +never(): VerificationMode
  +atLeast(minNumberOfInvocations: int): VerificationMode
  +atMost(maxNumberOfInvocations: int): VerificationMode
  +timeout(millis: long): VerificationMode
  +inOrder(mocks: Object...): InOrder
}

class BDDMockito <<BDD Style>> {
  +given(methodCall: T): BDDOngoingStubbing<T>
  +then(mock: T): Then<T>
  +willReturn(value: T): BDDStubber
  +willThrow(throwable: Throwable): BDDStubber
}

' Stubbing Classes
interface OngoingStubbing {
  +thenReturn(value: T): OngoingStubbing<T>
  +thenThrow(throwable: Throwable): OngoingStubbing<T>
  +thenAnswer(answer: Answer): OngoingStubbing<T>
  +thenCallRealMethod(): OngoingStubbing<T>
}

interface Answer {
  +answer(invocation: InvocationOnMock): Object
}

class InvocationOnMock {
  +getArgument(index: int): Object
  +getArguments(): Object[]
  +getMock(): Object
  +getMethod(): Method
}

' Verification Classes
interface VerificationMode {
  +verify(data: VerificationData): void
}

class InOrder {
  +verify(mock: T): T
  +verify(mock: T, mode: VerificationMode): T
}

' Argument Handling
class ArgumentMatchers <<Matchers>> {
  +any(): T
  +any(type: Class<T>): T
  +anyString(): String
  +anyInt(): int
  +anyBoolean(): boolean
  +eq(value: T): T
  +isNull(): T
  +isNotNull(): T
}

class ArgumentCaptor <<Captor>> {
  +forClass(clazz: Class<T>): ArgumentCaptor<T>
  +capture(): T
  +getValue(): T
  +getAllValues(): List<T>
}

' Test Class
class MockitoMockTest <<TestClass>> {
  -mockDeal: Deal
  -mockUser: User
  -mockCommissionPlan: CommissionPlan
  +testBasicStubbing(): void
  +testMethodVerification(): void
  +testArgumentMatchers(): void
  +testArgumentCaptors(): void
  +testSpyObjects(): void
  +testInOrderVerification(): void
  +testBDDStyle(): void
}

' Mocking Relationships
MockitoMockTest ..> Mockito : uses
MockitoMockTest ..> BDDMockito : uses
MockitoMockTest ..> ArgumentMatchers : uses
MockitoMockTest ..> ArgumentCaptor : uses
MockitoMockTest ..> MockitoAnnotations : initializes mocks

MockitoMockTest --> Deal : mocks
MockitoMockTest --> User : mocks
MockitoMockTest --> CommissionPlan : mocks

Mockito ..> OngoingStubbing : returns
Mockito ..> InOrder : creates
BDDMockito ..> OngoingStubbing : returns

OngoingStubbing ..> Answer : uses
Answer ..> InvocationOnMock : receives

' Component Notes
note right of Deal
  <b>Domain Model - Real Object</b>
  The actual business entity that
  will be mocked in tests.

  Contains business logic and
  state that we want to control
  during testing.
end note

note left of Mockito
  <b>Core Mocking API</b>
  Central class providing static
  methods for:
  • Creating mocks
  • Stubbing behavior
  • Verifying interactions
  • Resetting mocks

  <b>Key Method Pattern:</b>
  when(mock.method()).thenReturn(value)
  verify(mock).method()
end note

note bottom of OngoingStubbing
  <b>Fluent Stubbing Interface</b>
  Allows chaining of return values:

  when(mock.getValue())
    .thenReturn(100)
    .thenReturn(200)
    .thenThrow(new Exception())

  Provides different stubbing options:
  • thenReturn - static value
  • thenThrow - exception
  • thenAnswer - dynamic behavior
  • thenCallRealMethod - spy behavior
end note

note right of ArgumentCaptor
  <b>Argument Capture</b>
  Captures arguments passed to
  mock methods for detailed
  verification:

  ArgumentCaptor<BigDecimal> captor
    = ArgumentCaptor.forClass(BigDecimal.class);
  verify(mock).setValue(captor.capture());

  BigDecimal captured = captor.getValue();
  assertTrue(captured.compareTo(
    new BigDecimal("1000")) > 0);
end note

note bottom of InOrder
  <b>Sequential Verification</b>
  Verifies methods were called
  in specific order:

  InOrder inOrder = inOrder(mock);
  inOrder.verify(mock).setStatus(OPEN);
  inOrder.verify(mock).setStatus(WON);

  Critical for state machines
  and workflow validation.
end note

note bottom of BDDMockito
  <b>BDD Style Testing</b>
  Behavior-Driven Development syntax
  makes tests read like specifications:

  <b>Given-When-Then:</b>
  given(mock.method()).willReturn(value);
  // when - execute behavior
  then(mock).should().method();

  More readable for stakeholders.
end note

note bottom of ArgumentMatchers
  <b>Flexible Matching</b>
  Matches arguments without exact values:

  • any() - any object
  • anyString() - any String
  • eq(value) - exact match
  • isNull() / isNotNull()

  <b>Rule:</b> If using matchers,
  ALL arguments must use matchers!

  when(mock.add(eq(0), any(Deal.class)))
end note

note right of MockitoMockTest
  <b>Test Class</b>
  Demonstrates all Mockito features:

  <b>@Mock</b> - Auto-create mocks
  <b>@Spy</b> - Partial mocking
  <b>@Captor</b> - Argument captors
  <b>@InjectMocks</b> - Inject mocks

  Uses @BeforeEach to initialize
  mocks with MockitoAnnotations.openMocks()
end note

note bottom of Answer
  <b>Custom Behavior</b>
  Implements dynamic mock responses:

  when(mock.calculate()).thenAnswer(
    invocation -> {
      int arg = invocation.getArgument(0);
      return arg * 2;
    }
  );

  Access to:
  • Method arguments
  • Mock object
  • Method metadata
end note

' Legend
legend right
  <b>Mockito Mocking Concepts:</b>

  <b>Mock:</b> Empty shell object that returns
  default values (null, 0, false) unless stubbed.
  Created with @Mock or mock(Class).

  <b>Spy:</b> Wraps real object. Calls real
  methods unless explicitly stubbed. Created
  with @Spy or spy(Object).

  <b>Stubbing:</b> Defining what mocks return
  when methods are called:
  when(mock.method()).thenReturn(value)

  <b>Verification:</b> Checking that methods
  were called with expected arguments:
  verify(mock).method(args)

  <b>Argument Matchers:</b> Flexible matching
  in stubbing/verification: any(), eq(), etc.

  <b>Argument Captors:</b> Capture arguments
  for detailed inspection after method calls.

  <b>InOrder:</b> Verify method call sequence
  matters for state machines and workflows.

  <b>Key Benefits:</b>
  • Isolate unit tests from dependencies
  • Control behavior of collaborators
  • Verify interactions occurred
  • Test error scenarios easily
  • Speed up tests (no real I/O)
  • Test code before dependencies exist

  <b>When to Use:</b>
  ✓ Testing business logic with dependencies
  ✓ Verifying method interactions
  ✓ Simulating error conditions
  ✓ Isolating from slow/external resources

  <b>When to Avoid:</b>
  ✗ Simple POJOs without dependencies
  ✗ Integration tests (use real objects)
  ✗ Over-mocking (test behavior, not impl)
endlegend

